{"slug":"math","pages":[{"names":["sqrt"],"summary":"The square root (complex for negative numbers).","body":["Works element by element. The square root of a negative number is imaginary: `sqrt(-4)` is `0 + 2i`. For the matrix square root, a matrix `X` with `X*X` equal to `A`, use `sqrtm`."],"syntax":[["y = sqrt(x)","The square root of each element of `x`."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"Squares, non-squares and a negative number","intro":"","code":"y = sqrt([4 9 2])\nz = sqrt(-4)","after":"","output":"y =\n\n    2.0000    3.0000    1.4142\n\nz =\n\n   0.0000 + 2.0000i","figures":[]},{"title":"The length of a vector","intro":"","code":"v = [3 4 12];\nlen = sqrt(sum(v.^2))","after":"","output":"len =\n\n    13","figures":[]}],"notes":[],"see":["power","nthroot","sqrtm","hypot"]},{"names":["exp"],"summary":"The exponential, e^x.","body":["Works element by element; `exp(1)` is Euler's number e. Of an imaginary argument it gives a point on the unit circle: `exp(1i*theta)` is `cos(theta) + 1i*sin(theta)`. For the matrix exponential use `expm`."],"syntax":[["y = exp(x)","e raised to each element of `x`."]],"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"examples":[{"title":"e, and its powers","intro":"","code":"e = exp(1)\nexp([0 1 2])","after":"","output":"e =\n\n    2.7183\n\nans =\n\n    1.0000    2.7183    7.3891","figures":[]},{"title":"Exponential decay","intro":"","code":"t = linspace(0, 5, 100);\nplot(t, exp(-t), t, exp(-t/2))\nlegend('e^{-t}', 'e^{-t/2}')\nxlabel('t')","after":"","output":"","figures":[{"id":1,"type":"figure","number":1,"name":"","numberTitle":true,"width":560.0,"height":420.0,"color":[0.94,0.94,0.94],"visible":true,"version":6,"windowStyle":"normal","axes":[{"id":2,"type":"axes","kind":"cartesian","geo":false,"position":[0.13,0.11,0.775,0.815],"xlim":[0.0,5.0],"ylim":[0.0,1.0],"zlim":[0.0,1.0],"xlimMode":"auto","ylimMode":"auto","zlimMode":"auto","clim":[0.0,1.0],"tight":{"X":false,"Y":false,"Z":false},"xscale":"linear","yscale":"linear","zscale":"l